The '''Penshaw Monument''' (officially the '''Earl of Durham's Monument''') is a [[memorial]] in the style of an [[ancient Greek temple]] on Penshaw Hill in the [[metropolitan borough]] of the [[City of Sunderland]], [[North East England]]. It is located near the village of [[Penshaw]], between the towns of [[Washington, Tyne and Wear|Washington]] and [[Houghton-le-Spring]] in [[Historic counties of England|historic]] [[County Durham]]. The monument was built between 1844 and 1845{{efn|name=built}} to commemorate [[John Lambton, 1st Earl of Durham]] (1792–1840), [[Governor-General]] of [[British North America]] and author of the [[Durham Report]] on the future governance of the American territories. Owned by the [[National Trust]] since 1939, it is a [[Listed building#Categories of listed building|Grade I listed structure]].

The monument was designed by [[John and Benjamin Green]] and built by Thomas Pratt of [[Bishopwearmouth]] using local [[gritstone]] at a cost of around £6000; the money was raised by [[Subscription (finance)|subscription]]. On 28 August 1844, while it was partially complete, its foundation stone was laid by [[Thomas Dundas, 2nd Earl of Zetland|the 2nd Earl of Zetland]] in a [[Masonic ritual and symbolism|Masonic ceremony]] which drew tens of thousands of spectators. Based on the [[Temple of Hephaestus]] in [[Athens]], it is a [[tetrastyle]] temple of the [[Doric order]], with eighteen columns—seven along its longer sides and four along its shorter ones—and no roof or [[cella]] (inner chamber).

One column contains a [[Stairs#Spiral and helical stairs|spiral staircase]] leading to a [[parapet]]ed walkway along the [[entablature]]. This staircase was closed to the public in 1926 after a 15-year-old boy fell to his death from the top of the monument. The structure fell into disrepair in the 1930s and was fenced off, then repaired in 1939. It has since undergone further restoration, including extensive work in 1979 during which its western side was dismantled. [[Floodlight|Floodlit]] at night since 1988, it is often illuminated in different colours to mark special occasions. The National Trust began to offer supervised tours of the walkway in 2011.

Penshaw Monument is a local [[landmark]], visible from up to {{convert|50|mi|order=flip}} away. It appears on the [[Crest (sports)|crest]] of [[Sunderland A.F.C.]] and is viewed nationally as a symbol of the North East. It has been praised for the grandeur, simplicity and symbolic significance of its design, especially when seen from a distance. However, critics have said it is poorly constructed and lacks purpose; nineteenth-century architectural journals condemned its lack of a roof and the hollowness of its columns and walls. It features no depiction of the man it honours, and has been widely described as a [[folly]].

==Location==
[[File:Penshaw Monument map.png|thumb|alt=Map of the area around the monument|The National Trust landholding includes Penshaw Wood and Dawson's Plantation.]]
Penshaw Monument stands on the south-western edge of the [[summit]] of Penshaw Hill,{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=4}}{{efn|"Penshaw" is often spelled ''Painshaw'' or ''Pensher'' in older sources.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|page=6}} Its etymology is uncertain, but it may be derived from the [[Proto-Celtic language|Celtic]] words *''penn'' and *''cerr'', meaning "the head of the rocks".<ref>{{cite encyclopedia |last=Mills |first=A. D. |encyclopedia=A Dictionary of British Place Names |title=Penshaw  |url=https://www.oxfordreference.com/view/10.1093/acref/9780199609086.001.0001/acref-9780199609086-e-14638 |access-date=17 July 2020 |date=2011 |publisher=[[Oxford University Press]] |isbn= 9780191739446 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> }} an isolated {{convert|136|m|adj=on}} [[Hillock|knoll]] formed by the erosion of an [[escarpment]] of the Durham [[Magnesian Limestone]] Plateau.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pages=7, 9, 10}} The [[National Trust]] landholding at the site totals {{convert|18|ha}},{{sfn|Newman|2019|p=2}} including {{convert|12|ha}} of [[deciduous]] [[woodland]] to the west of the monument.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=4}}{{efn|The exact extent of the landholding is marked on the maps in {{harvnb|Middleton|2010|loc=between pp. 3–4 and 60–61}}.}} The woodland is split into Dawson's Plantation in the north and Penshaw Wood in the south.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=4}} Both the summit of the hill and the woodland are considered [[Site of Nature Conservation Interest|Sites of Nature Conservation Interest]] by [[Sunderland City Council]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=4}}

The monument's car park is accessible from Chester Road (the [[A roads in Zone 1 of the Great Britain numbering scheme|A183]]); three [[footpath]]s lead from the car park to the monument, which can also be reached from Grimestone Bank in the north-west and Hill Lane in the south.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|page=4}} The [[National Heritage List for England]] gives the monument's statutory address as Hill Lane,<ref name="he"/> but Sunderland City Council lists the property as located on Chester Road.<ref name="mysunderland">{{Cite web|date=2022|title=Penshaw Monument|url=https://www.mysunderland.co.uk/article/18747/Penshaw-Monument|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240514224717/https://mysunderland.co.uk/article/18747/Penshaw-Monument|archive-date=14 May 2024|access-date=2022-07-26|website=MySunderland|publisher=[[Sunderland City Council]]|language=en}}</ref> There have been few changes to the site since the monument's construction, although signs, fences and floodlights have been added, and footpaths have been improved by the National Trust.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=48}} There is an [[Ordnance Survey]] [[Triangulation station#United Kingdom|trig point]] to the west of the monument.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=48}}

The site receives over 60,000 visitors every year;<ref name="henderson2019">{{cite news |last1=Henderson |first1=Tony |title=Help for one of the North East's most recognisable landmarks |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/help-one-north-easts-most-16736148 |access-date=3 July 2020 |work=[[Evening Chronicle|Chronicle Live]] |date=12 August 2019}}</ref> people come to visit the monument, admire the views or engage in [[Walking in the United Kingdom|walking]], jogging or photography.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pp=51, 53}} The Trust has placed a [[Geocaching|geocache]] at the site.<ref>{{cite web |title=Go geocaching at Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/penshaw-monument/features/go-geocaching-at-penshaw-monument |publisher=[[National Trust]] |access-date=7 August 2020}}</ref> The Penshaw Bowl, an [[egg rolling|Easter egg rolling]] competition for children, takes place on the hill every [[Maundy Thursday]];{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=2}} this tradition is over a century old.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Davies |first1=Hannah |title=Easter echoes of our Pagan past |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=13 April 2006 |page=46}}</ref> The hill is also popular for [[Guy Fawkes Night|Bonfire Night]] and New Year celebrations.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=6}}

The surrounding area was formerly industrialised, but is now mainly [[Arable land|arable farmland]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|page=7}} The site is in the [[Shiney Row]] [[Wards and electoral divisions of the United Kingdom|ward]];<ref name="hansard">{{Cite Hansard|house=House of Commons |title=Home Affairs |url=https://hansard.parliament.uk/Commons/1997-05-19/debates/8447b00c-cca9-476d-bbae-5601dc08b4da/HomeAffairs#contribution-5e9c93df-c1f8-418f-a753-ee3d224dc85a |date=19 May 1997 |column=436 |speaker=[[Fraser Kemp]] |position=MP for [[Houghton and Washington East (UK Parliament constituency)|Houghton and Washington East]] }}</ref> it is south-west of [[Sunderland]], north-east of [[Chester-le-Street]], south-east of [[Washington, Tyne and Wear|Washington]] and north of [[Houghton-le-Spring]].<ref name="map">{{Google maps |url= https://www.google.com/maps/place/Penshaw+Monument,+Houghton+le+Spring,+Sunderland+SR4+9JX/@54.8776233,-1.475383,12z |title=Penshaw Monument |access-date= 20 June 2020}}</ref> To the north is the [[WWT Washington|Washington Wetland Centre]], managed by the [[Wildfowl & Wetlands Trust]]; to the south is [[Herrington Country Park]].{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} The monument is visible from {{convert|50|mi|order=flip|abbr=on}} away on a sunny day<ref name="gillan">{{cite news |last1=Gillan |first1=Tony |title=The story of Penshaw Monument – the real reason why Sunderland has a huge mock Greek monument on a hill and its links to the Freemasons |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/heritage-and-retro/heritage/story-penshaw-monument-real-reason-why-sunderland-has-huge-mock-greek-monument-hill-and-its-links-freemasons-2536702 |access-date=18 June 2020 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=13 June 2020}}</ref> and can be seen from the [[A1 road (Great Britain)|A1 road]];{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=51}} from the hill, it is sometimes possible to see the [[Cheviot Hills]] in Northumberland and the central tower of [[Durham Cathedral]],<ref>{{cite web |title=Things to see and do at Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/penshaw-monument/features/things-to-see-and-do-at-penshaw-monument |publisher=[[National Trust]] |access-date=10 July 2020}}</ref> as well as the sea.<ref name="henderson2005">{{cite news |last1=Henderson |first1=Tony |title=Well-loved historic tribute fit for a lord |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=10 October 2005 |page=18}}</ref>

===History of the site===
There is evidence that Penshaw Hill may have been an [[Iron Age]] [[hillfort]]: the remains of what may be [[Rampart (fortification)|ramparts]] have been identified at the site, and the expansive views from the hill would have made it a strategically advantageous location for a fort.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=15}} In March 1644, during the [[First English Civil War]], the hill served as an [[Military camp|encampment]] for an army of Scottish [[Covenanters]] who fled there after a failed attack on [[Newcastle upon Tyne|Newcastle]] before the [[Battle of Boldon Hill]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pp=21–22}} The hill is associated with the local legend of the [[Lambton Worm]]; a [[folk song]] written by [[C. M. Leumane]] in 1867 describes the worm wrapping itself "ten times roond Pensha Hill".{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=17}}{{efn|{{harvtxt|Middleton|2010|p=18}} suggests that the ramparts of the hillfort may have formed ridges on the hill which caused it to be associated with the legend of the worm. She compares the worm to [[The dragon (Beowulf)|the dragon]] in ''[[Beowulf]]'' and suggests it may have been conceived as the guardian of an ancient site located on the hill. In earlier versions of the legend, however, the hill in question is [[Worm Hill]] in the nearby village of [[Fatfield]].<ref name="henderson2005"/>}} The hill is the site of an 18th-century limestone [[quarry]] on Dawson's Plantation, which is a designated [[Site of Special Scientific Interest]];{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pp=4, 6}} farming and quarrying on nearby land continued after the monument's construction.{{sfn|Newman|2019|p=5}}

The landholding is on the north-eastern edge of the historical [[township (England)|township]] of [[Penshaw]];{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=21}} the original village of Old Penshaw is approximately {{convert|0.5|km}} from the monument.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=24}} After Penshaw Colliery opened in 1792, a new [[pit village]] was established to the south-west of the original village – it was known as New Penshaw.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=24}} The earliest record of Penshaw is in the [[Boldon Book]] of 1183, where it is described as being leased by [[William Basset (12th-century judge)|William Basset]] from a Jordan de Escoland, later Jordan de Dalton.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=20}} Other former landowners of the [[vill]] include the [[Bowes-Lyon]] and [[Earl of Durham|Lambton]] families.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=20}} The land on which the monument stands was eventually passed to the Vane-Tempest-Stewart estate and became the property of [[Charles Vane, 3rd Marquess of Londonderry]], who gifted it as the site of the structure.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pp=20–21}}{{efn|According to an 1848 [[tithe map]], the hill officially remained the Marquess's property for a period after the monument's construction.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=25}}}}

{{Wide image|Penshaw from Herrington Park (cropped).jpg|1250px|alt=A hill partly covered in trees, with a Greek temple at the summit|Moveable panorama of the monument seen from [[Herrington Country Park]]}}

==Conception and construction==
===Background===
{{main|John Lambton, 1st Earl of Durham}}
[[File:John George Lambton, 1st Earl of Durham by Thomas Phillips.jpg|thumb|alt=Portrait of the Earl of Durham|[[John Lambton, 1st Earl of Durham|The 1st Earl of Durham]] ]]
John George Lambton (born 1792) was the son of [[William Henry Lambton]] and Lady Anne Barbara Frances Villiers. He attended [[Eton College]], then joined the [[10th Royal Hussars]] in 1809. Lambton became [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] for [[County Durham (UK Parliament constituency)|County Durham]] in 1813; politically, he had a reputation for [[radicalism (historical)|radicalism]] and proposing [[electoral reform]], earning him the nickname "Radical Jack". In 1828 he was raised to the [[Peerages in the United Kingdom|peerage]], becoming Baron Durham. In 1830 Durham was made [[Lord Privy Seal]] in [[Whig government, 1830–1834|Earl Grey's cabinet]] and was charged with producing a draft of the bill that became the [[Reform Act 1832]]. He resigned his position in 1833, and was created [[Earl of Durham]] shortly afterwards. He was a [[Freemasonry|Freemason]] and became Deputy Grand Master of the [[United Grand Lodge of England]] in 1834.<ref name="odnb">{{Cite ODNB|id=15947|title=Lambton, John George}}</ref>

The [[second Melbourne ministry]] appointed him [[List of ambassadors of the United Kingdom to Russia|Ambassador to Russia]] in 1835; he spent two years in the post. In 1838, Lord Durham agreed to become [[Governor-General]] of [[British North America]], and was sent to the [[British colonization of the Americas|British colony]] of [[Lower Canada]] (now [[Quebec]]) to deal with [[Lower Canada Rebellion|rebellions]] in the region. After a failed attempt to exile rebel leaders to [[Bermuda]], he resigned from the post and left Canada after five months. His ''[[Durham Report|Report on the Affairs of British North America]]'' (1839) recommended that Lower Canada merge with the English-speaking province of [[Upper Canada]] to anglicise its [[French Canadians|French-speaking]] inhabitants; it also advocated limited [[Self-governing colony|self-government]] for the colony.<ref name="odnb"/>

After an illness lasting several months, thought to be [[tuberculosis]], he died on 28 July 1840 in [[Cowes]], [[Isle of Wight]].<ref name="odnb"/> On 3 August, his body was taken in his own yacht to Sunderland, then in a [[steamship]] to [[Lambton Castle]].{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|p=27}} His funeral took place on 10 August, and was attended by over 300 Freemasons; they wanted to perform a [[Masonic ritual and symbolism|Masonic ceremony]] for the occasion, but were asked not to do so by Durham's family.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|p=28}} The ceremony began at the castle, where the Earl [[Lying in state|lay in state]] in the dining room.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|p=28}} A [[Funeral procession|procession]], consisting of around 450 people in carriages and hundreds more on foot, then conveyed the body to the [[parish church]] of [[St Mary and St Cuthbert, Chester-le-Street]], where Durham was buried in his family vault.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|pp=29–30}} A large crowd gathered to observe the procession; its size has been estimated as between 30,000 and 50,000 people.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|p=29}} The Marquess of Londonderry, a political opponent of Durham's, travelled from London to act as a [[pallbearer]] at the funeral.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|pp=28, 30}}

===1840–1842: proposals and selection of the site===
[[File:Lambton Arms Public House Chester-le-Street - geograph.org.uk - 127684.jpg|thumb|left|alt=A pub with yellow walls on a street corner|The Lambton Arms in 2006]]
At a meeting at the Lambton Arms [[pub]] in Chester-le-Street on 19 August 1840, it was decided to form a Provisional Committee to raise funds for a monument to the Earl by [[Subscription (finance)|subscription]].{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|p=38}} The next day, at the Assembly Rooms in Newcastle, a committee of 33 men was formed for that purpose; it included the [[mayor]]s of Newcastle and [[Gateshead]].{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1841|p=38}} The chairman of the committee was [[Henry John Spearman]].<ref name="nj31augp3">{{cite news |title=Monument to the Late John George, Earl of Durham |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000243/18440831/006/0003 |work=Newcastle Journal |date=31 August 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The following motion was put forward by [[William Ord (1781–1855)|William Ord]] MP:

{{blockquote|text=That the distinguished services rendered to his country by the late John George Lambton, Earl of Durham, as an honest, able, and patriotic satesman, and as the enlightened and liberal friend to the improvement of the people in morals, education, and scientific acquirements, combined with his unceasing exercise of the most active benevolence, and of the other private virtues which adorned his character, render it the sacred duty of his fellow-countrymen to erect a public monument to perpetuate the memory of his services, his talents, and his virtues, and to act as an incitement to others to follow his bright example.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Late Earl of Durham |work=[[The Times]] |date=24 August 1840 |page=3 |url=https://www.thetimes.com/archive/article/1840-08-24/3/4.html}}</ref>}}

Around £500 ({{inflation|UK|500|1840|fmt=eq|cursign=£|r=-2}}) was pledged at this meeting. There were initially disagreements about the site of the monument: proposals included Durham, the Earl's [[territorial designation]]; Chester-le-Street, his burial place; Sunderland, where he had trading connections; and Newcastle, due to its size.<ref name="carlisle"/> In a letter to the ''[[Durham Chronicle]]'', published in October 1840, an anonymous subscriber to the monument urged that members of the public be allowed to submit designs, and that the final design be chosen by subscribers.<ref name="dc17oct">{{cite news |title=The Durham Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0001653/18401017/033/0003 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=17 October 1840 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The letter warned of a "vicious system of jobbing" in which the exercise of private influence led to the adoption of inferior designs, which it claimed had influenced the planning of other monuments such as [[Nelson's Column]].<ref name="dc17oct"/>

At another meeting at the Bridge Hotel in Sunderland on 28 January 1842,<ref>{{cite news |title=Durham Testimonial |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000612/18420204/048/0003 |work=Durham County Advertiser |date=4 February 1842 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> [[William Hutt (politician)|William Hutt]] MP proposed that "the monument should be of an architectural character", and suggested Penshaw Hill as a location because it was the Earl's property,{{efn|Although Hutt described Penshaw Hill as Durham's property, {{harvtxt|Middleton|2010|p=25}} writes that the hill itself was owned by the Marquess of Londonderry, and the Earl owned the land surrounding it.}} and the monument would be visible from much of [[County Durham]] and close to the [[East Coast Main Line]].{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=15}} Durham's wife had expressed support for this site before her death.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=15}} According to ''[[The Times]]'', "a more suitable spot for the erection of a monument to the late lamented Earl could not have been selected".<ref name="times1844"/> Hutt hoped to erect a [[statue]] of Durham, and read out a letter from a sculptor who had offered to make one.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=15}} By this point, around £3000 ({{inflation|UK|3000|1842|r=-3|fmt=eq|cursign=£}}) had been subscribed.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=15}}{{efn|For a list of subscribers as of late 1840 and the amounts they gave, see {{harvnb|''Local Collections''|1841|pp=63–64}}.}} More money was later raised by a London-based committee.{{sfn|Fordyce|1857|p=567}}

===1842–1843: selection of the design===
The committee sought advice from the [[Royal Institute of British Architects]] in London.<ref name="dc15july1842">{{cite news |title=The Late Earl of Durham's Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0001653/18420715/060/0004?browse=true |access-date=20 July 2020 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=15 July 1842 |page=4| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Its secretary, [[Thomas Leverton Donaldson]], advised approaching five or six skilled architects named by the Institute privately, rather than advertising publicly for designs.<ref name="dc15july1842"/> Donaldson told Hutt that if a public call for designs was made, the most skilled architects would not compete.<ref name="dc15july1842"/> The Institute surveyed Penshaw Hill and produced instructions to the architects, which described the hill and indicated subscribers' preference for a [[column]].<ref name="dc15july1842"/> The instructions stated that the project could not cost more than £3000.<ref name="dc15july1842"/> Six of the designs submitted to the committee were exhibited at the institute's premises in London before they were seen in the north.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=89}}{{sfn|''Art Union''|1844}} These were all either columns or [[obelisk]]s, each topped with a statue of the Earl.{{sfn|''Art Union''|1844}} At a meeting in Sunderland on 8 July 1842, subscribers examined proposals by seven architects.<ref name="dc15july1842"/> These were:

[[File:Ναός του Ηφαίστου.jpg|thumb|alt=A Greek temple|Both Cory's column and the final temple design were based on the [[Temple of Hephaestus]], Athens.]]

*John Augustus Cory—Two designs: a column in the style of [[Italianate architecture]]; and a column based on those of the [[Temple of Hephaestus]]{{efn|In older sources the Temple of Hephaestus is called the "Temple of Theseus" or the "Theseon".}}
*Thomas Leverton Donaldson—Design unknown
*[[Harvey Lonsdale Elmes]]—Two designs: a Grecian column topped by a temple containing an urn; and a column with projecting balconies in imitation of a Roman [[rostral column]]
*[[Charles Fowler]]—A [[Norman architecture|Norman]] column, similar to those in the [[nave]] of Durham Cathedral
*Arthur Mee—A column; details unknown
*[[John Buonarotti Papworth]]—An [[obelisk]], with a bronze statue and [[sarcophagus]] at the front
*Robert Wallace—A Doric column, {{convert|13|ft|order=flip}} in diameter, based on those of the [[Temple of Olympian Zeus, Agrigento]] and topped with a podium and a metal tripod<ref name="dc15july1842"/>

Some present at the meeting were unhappy that the preference for a column had been expressed—one felt that "it would have been better to have left the genius of the artists unfettered", and another wished to receive more designs before a decision was made.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=107}} There was some consternation that the building of the monument was not taking place as quickly as had been anticipated.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=107}} The ''Durham Chronicle'' disapproved of the proposed designs, writing that "a column, standing in solitary nakedness, is a palpable absurdity".<ref name="dc22july">{{cite news |title=The Durham Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001653/18420722/030/0002 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=22 July 1842 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> It criticised the designs of [[Grey's Monument]] and Nelson's Column, believing them to belong to "the candlestick style of monumental architecture", and wrote that the monument to the Earl of Durham should be "lofty, massive, durable, and distinctive—simple in its features, and grand in its general effect".<ref name="dc22july"/>

On 8 November 1842, an executive committee with the power to choose a design and begin construction of the monument was formed in Newcastle.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1843|p=182}} In May 1843, the committee met to consider new designs that it had received, and decided to recommend [[John and Benjamin Green]]'s proposal of a Grecian Doric temple to subscribers.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Durham Memorial |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000243/18430506/003/0002 |work=Newcastle Journal |date=6 May 1843|page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> By July, the design had been officially selected.{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1843|p=253}} The Greens were father and son, and also designed Grey's Monument and the [[Theatre Royal, Newcastle|Theatre Royal]] in Newcastle.<ref name="henderson2005"/> The initial design was in the style of the [[Paestum#The three Greek Temples|temples of Paestum]], with an arrangement of four by six columns; this was later changed to one based on the Temple of Hephaestus.<ref name="dca30aug"/>{{efn|According to the ''Carlisle Journal'', a design based on the Temple of Hephaestus was suggested by [[Charles Grey, 2nd Earl Grey]],<ref name="carlisle"/> former Prime Minister and Durham's father-in-law. }} It was envisaged that the hill would become an enclosed [[pleasure garden]] after the monument's construction.<ref name="dca30aug"/>

===1844: construction and foundation stone ceremony===
[[File:Durham Monument.png|thumb|upright=1.3|left|alt=An engraving of a large crowd surrounding a partly complete temple structure, which is topped with flags and missing its entablature|An [[engraving]] of the ceremony published in ''[[The Illustrated London News]]'', showing the monument in an unfinished state]]
The stone used in the construction was a gift from the Marquess of Londonderry; it came from his quarries in Penshaw, about {{convert|1|mi|order=flip}} from the hill.<ref name="times1844"/>{{efn|A theory exists that some stones used in the construction came from a Roman river crossing at [[South Hylton]]—there is no evidence for this.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=43}}}} In a letter to subscribers, the Marquess explained his decision to provide the stone: "it has afforded me great satistfaction in a very humble manner to aid in recording my admiration of [the Earl of Durham's] talents and abilities, however I may have differed with him on public or political subjects".<ref>{{cite news |title=The Marquis of Londonderry and the Durham Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001653/18440621/045/0003 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=21 June 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> [[Lime (material)|Lime]] used in the construction was made in [[Lime kiln|kilns]] owned by the Earl of Durham, located in the nearby village of [[Newbottle, Tyne and Wear|Newbottle]]; sand was obtained from a [[Digging#Borrow pit|sand pit]] at the foot of Penshaw Hill.<ref name="times1844"/> The materials were brought up the hill by a temporary [[Spiral (railway)|spiral railway]].<ref name="times1844"/> Holes on the stone blocks of the monument's [[stylobate]] indicate that they were transported with a lifting device called a [[Lewis (lifting appliance)|lewis]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}}

In January and February 1844, an [[invitation to tender]] for the monument's construction was placed in the ''Durham Chronicle'' and ''Newcastle Courant'' newspapers.<ref name="tender1">{{cite news |title=Testimonial to the Late Earl of Durham |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001653/18440126/012/0001 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=26 January 1844 |page=1 |via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref><ref name="tender2">{{cite news |title=Testimonial to the Late Earl of Durham |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000085/18440202/001/0001 |work=Newcastle Courant |date=2 February 1844 |page=1 |via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The deadline to submit tenders was initially 15 February, but was later extended to 1 March.<ref name="tender1"/><ref name="tender2"/> By 15 March the builder Thomas Pratt of [[Bishopwearmouth]], Sunderland, had been awarded the contract;<ref name="dc15march">{{cite news |title=Local Intelligence |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0001653/18440315/036/0003 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=15 March 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> little is known about him.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=38}} By March, operations to clear the ground at the site had begun;<ref name="dc15march"/> an invitation to tender for the transportation of the stone from the quarry to Penshaw Hill appeared in the ''Durham Chronicle'' on 29 March.<ref>{{cite news |title=Testimonial to the Memory of the Late Earl of Durham |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001653/18440329/025/0002 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=29 March 1844 |page=2 |via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> By May, a trench for the monument's foundations had been dug.<ref>{{cite news |title=The Lord Durham Memorial |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000243/18440525/006/0003 |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|Newcastle Journal]] |date=25 May 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

[[File:Thomas Dundas, 2nd Earl of Zetland.png|thumb|upright=0.7|alt=Caricature of the Earl of Zetland|A ''[[Vanity Fair (UK magazine)|Vanity Fair]]'' caricature of the Earl of Zetland]]

The [[Cornerstone|foundation stone]] of the monument was laid on 28 August 1844 by [[Thomas Dundas, 2nd Earl of Zetland]], [[Grand Master (Masonic)|Grand Master]] of the United Grand Lodge of England. The construction had been underway for several months:<ref name="times1844"/> all the columns had been built to around half of their final height, except two at the front of the structure.<ref name="dca30aug">{{cite news |title=Monument to the Late Earl of Durham |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0000612/18440830/027/0003 |work=Durham County Advertiser |date=30 August 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The monument's [[scaffolding]] was adorned with flags for the ceremony.<ref name="dc30aug1844">{{cite news |title=Laying the Foundation Stone of the Monument to the Late Earl of Durham on Pensher Hill |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001653/18440830/042/0003 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=30 August 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The [[Great North of England Railway]] Company organised special trains from Sunderland, Newcastle, [[Durham, England|Durham]] and [[South Shields]] to bring spectators to the event, and galleries were erected on either side of the monument to allow them to watch the ceremony<ref name="times1844"/>—most of those in the galleries were women.<ref name="dca30aug"/> The ''Durham Chronicle'' estimated that between 15,000 and 20,000 spectators were present;<ref name="dc30aug1844"/> the ''[[Durham County Advertiser]]'' reported at least 30,000.<ref name="dca30aug"/>

A [[pavilion]] was put up in a field to the south of Penshaw Hill to accommodate the Freemasons present,<ref name="times1844"/> including 400 members of the [[Provincial Grand Lodges (UGLE)|Provincial Grand Lodge]] of Durham.<ref name="henderson2005"/> At around 1:30&nbsp;pm, the Freemasons formed a [[procession]] and ascended the hill, accompanied by a [[marching band]] and the monument's building committee.{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1845|p=102}}{{efn|A full list of those who were part of the procession is given in {{harvnb|''Local Collections''|1845|p=102}}.}} The [[Masonic lodge officers#Treasurer|Grand Treasurer]] placed a [[Vial|phial]] containing Victorian coins into a cavity in the foundation stone, which was then covered with a [[Commemorative plaque|brass plate]] bearing an inscription that dedicated the monument to the Earl of Durham.<ref name="times1844"/>{{efn|For the full text of the inscription, see {{harvnb|''Local Collections''|1845|p=102}}. It has since been eroded; the National Trust has installed a brushed steel plaque on the monument bearing the same text.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pp=27, 59}}}} Zetland then spread [[Mortar (masonry)|mortar]] on the stone with a silver [[trowel]], specially engraved for the occasion.<ref name="times1844"/> A second stone was then lowered on top of it as the band played "[[Rule, Britannia!]]",{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1845|p=102}} and Zetland used a [[Freemasonry#Ritual and symbolism|plummet, level and square]] to adjust the upper stone before strewing it with [[Cornerstone#Freemasonry|corn, wine and oil]].<ref name="times1844"/> [[The Reverend]] Robert Green of Newcastle said a prayer and the Freemasons examined the plans of the monument before returning to their pavilion as the band played "[[God Save the Queen]]".<ref name="times1844"/>{{sfn|''Local Collections''|1845|p=102}} ''The Times'' described the event thus:

{{blockquote|text=
[A] more animated and picturesque scene was perhaps never witnessed in this part of the country.{{nbsp}}... The gorgeous insignia of the masonic brethren brilliantly reflected the rays of an almost vertical sun, the various banners fluttering in the gentle breeze, the gay dresses of the ladies, and the vast assemblage of spectators on every side, formed altogether a magnificent spectacle.<ref name="times1844"/>
}}

That evening, two dinners were held in Sunderland to celebrate the event: one at the Wheatsheaf in [[Monkwearmouth]] and another at the Bridge Hotel.<ref name="dca30aug"/> The former was attended by many of the Freemasons who had participated in the ceremony; the latter accommodated many members of the gentry.<ref name="dca30aug"/> A dispute arose at one of these dinners when the vice-chairman, a [[Liberal Party (UK)|Liberal]] solicitor called A. J. Moore, refused to take part in a [[toast (honor)|toast]] in honour of the Marquess of Londonderry, a [[Tory]].<ref name="banquet">{{cite news |title=The Durham Memorial and the Sunderland Banquet |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000243/18440831/004/0002 |work=Newcastle Journal |date=31 August 1844 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Moore left the room, and the toast was drunk in his absence.<ref name="banquet"/> The ''[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|Newcastle Journal]]'' condemned Moore's behaviour as a "brutal display of corrupt feeling, unmanly resentment, and base ingratitude".<ref name="banquet"/> In September 1844, one of the monument's architects threatened legal action against J. C. Farrow, who had announced the publication of a [[Lithography|lithograph]] depicting the structure.<ref name="lithograph">{{cite news |title=Lord Durham's Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0001653/18440906/027/0003 |work=Durham Chronicle |date=6 September 1844 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> Green claimed that the monument—which was not yet finished—could not be depicted without reference to the architectural plans, and that the lithograph infringed his "rights of copy and design".<ref name="lithograph"/> The ''Durham Chronicle'' called Green's claim "utterly preposterous and absurd".<ref name="lithograph"/> In October, the ''[[Carlisle Journal]]'' reported that only one of the monument's columns had been topped with its [[capital (architecture)|capital]], and that the structure was expected to be completed in 1845.<ref name="carlisle"/> The total cost of the construction was approximately £6000 ({{inflation|UK|6000|1844|fmt=eq|cursign=£|r=-2}}).{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}}{{sfn|Harper|1922|p=131}}

==Subsequent history==
===1880s to 1920s: early damage and fatal accident===
[[File:Penshaw Monument, Doorway (geograph 2457282).jpg|thumb|left|upright=0.7|alt=A door in a stone column, covered with a metal grate|The door to the walkway was locked after Scott's death.]]
On 29 May 1889, the [[Society of Antiquaries of Newcastle upon Tyne]] was told of damage to the monument: many of the stones forming its stylobate had been removed and rolled down Penshaw Hill.{{sfn|Society of Antiquaries|1891|p=52}} The stonework was repaired in the 1920s after it became cracked.<ref name="echo1936">{{cite news |title=Penshaw Monument Damage |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000640/19360902/055/0002 |work=[[Sunderland Echo|Sunderland Echo and Shipping Gazette]] |date=2 September 1936 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref>

It was once possible to pay a penny for the key to the staircase to the top of the structure.<ref name="echo1953">{{cite news |title=Wearside Echoes |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000640/19530813/018/0002 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=13 August 1953 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> This ended after a fatal accident:<ref name="echo1953"/> on 5 April ([[Easter Monday]]) 1926, Temperley Arthur Scott, a 15-year-old [[Apprenticeship|apprentice]] [[Bricklayer|mason]] from [[Fatfield]], fell to his death from the top of Penshaw Monument.<ref name="echo-fall">{{cite news |title=Penshaw Fatality |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000289/19260407/238/0010 |work=[[Sunderland Echo|Sunderland Daily Echo and Shipping Gazette]] |date=7 April 1926 |page=10| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> There were around 20 people at the top when he fell.<ref name="echo-fall"/> He had ascended the structure with three of his friends; the group had climbed around the top twice and were attempting to do so a third time.<ref name="echo-fall"/> Scott tried to climb over a [[pediment]] to cross between the two walkways when he stumbled and fell {{convert|70|ft|order=flip}}.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=2}}<ref name="echo-fall"/> A doctor pronounced him dead at the scene.<ref name="echo-fall"/> A [[police officer]] told an [[inquest]] at the Primitive Methodist Chapel in Fatfield that it was usual for people to visit the top of the monument on [[Public holidays in the United Kingdom|public holidays]].<ref name="echo-fall"/> He said the stonework at the top of the pediment was worn, suggesting that many people had scaled it.<ref name="echo-fall"/> The deputy [[coroner]] declared a verdict of [[accidental death]] and recommended either that spiked railings be put on the pediments, or that the door to the staircase be permanently locked.<ref name="echo-fall"/> The door was kept locked from then on; after repeated break-ins it was sealed with [[cement]], and later bricks.<ref name="echo1953"/>

===1930s to 1970s: National Trust restoration===
{{multiple image|direction=vertical|width=220|image1=Penshaw Monument Circa 1979, during underpinning.jpg|image2=Penshaw Monument Circa 1979.jpg|footer=During repairs, {{circa|1979}}|alt1=Penshaw Monument covered in scaffolding, with parts of its entablature missing|alt2=A view of the same structure from the west}}
At a conference organised by the [[Campaign to Protect Rural England|Council for the Preservation of Rural England]] in [[Leamington Spa]] in summer 1937, J. E. McCutcheon of [[Seaham]] Town Council spoke about the need to protect tourist destinations in County Durham.<ref name="echo19oct39">{{cite news |title=Wearside Echoes |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000640/19391019/040/0002 |work=[[Sunderland Echo|Sunderland Echo and Shipping Gazette]] |date=19 October 1939 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> McCutcheon's comments interested B. L. Thompson, who was attending the conference on behalf of the National Trust; the two men began corresponding.<ref name="echo19oct39"/> As a result, the Trust agreed to take over Penshaw Monument from [[John Lambton, 5th Earl of Durham]] on the condition that [[Covenant (law)|covenants]] be imposed on Cocken Wood, an area of woodland near [[Finchale Priory]].<ref name="echo19oct39"/> The monument became the Trust's property in September 1939.<ref name="gillan"/> In April 1950 it was classed as a [[Listed building#Categories of listed building|Grade I listed building]] on the National Heritage List for England; its official name is the Earl of Durham's Monument.<ref name="he"/> Grade I buildings make up only 2.5% of listed buildings, and are described by [[Historic England]] as "of exceptional interest".<ref>{{cite web |title=Listed Buildings |url=https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/what-is-designation/listed-buildings/ |publisher=[[Historic England]] |access-date=24 July 2020}}</ref>

In 1936 the ''[[Sunderland Echo]] and Shipping Gazette'' reported that multiple large stones had fallen from the monument and a fence had been erected around it<ref name="echo1936"/>—the fence was wooden and covered in [[barbed wire]], and was still there in 1938.<ref>{{cite news |title=Penshaw Monument—Danger of Becoming a Ruin |work=[[Sunderland Echo|Sunderland Echo and Shipping Gazette]]|url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000640/19380610/141/0007 |date=10 June 1938 |page=7| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> By July 1939 repair work was being carried out, and scaffolding was present around some of the monument's columns.<ref>{{cite news |title=Restoration |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000640/19390722/053/0002 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] and Shipping Gazette |date=22 July 1939 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> It was not damaged during the [[Second World War]], despite raids on Houghton-le-Spring during [[the Blitz]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=33}} In 1942 it was struck by lightning; this caused damage to the top of the column containing the staircase.<ref>{{cite news |title=Damage to Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0000640/19420420/054/0005?browse=true |access-date=19 July 2020 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=20 April 1942 |page=5 | via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription }}</ref> This damage—a hole at the top of the column and a fissure extending half its length—was still visible a decade later.<ref name="echo1953"/>

In 1951 the ''Sunderland Echo'' reported that children had unsealed the door to the staircase and climbed the monument to search for pigeons' eggs; the National Trust employed a local builder to reseal it.<ref>{{cite news |title='Faux pas' by council |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0000640/19511122/098/0007 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=22 November 1951 |page=7| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> In 1959 the [[National Coal Board]] repaired the monument after it was damaged by subsistence caused by mining: its northern, western and southern sides had become cracked, and part of the walkway had detached and overhung the interior.<ref name="times1959">{{cite news |title=Subsistence threatens Durham landmark |url=https://www.thetimes.com/archive/article/1959-10-19/12/14.html |work=[[The Times]] |date=19 October 1959 |page=12}}</ref> Stone blocks were replaced with concrete slabs with stone facings.<ref name="times1959"/> Because of further [[Settlement (structural)|settlement]], Penshaw Monument was [[Underpinning|underpinned]] in 1978.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} The next year the western side was taken apart, and damaged [[lintel]]s were replaced with ones made of [[reinforced concrete]];{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} the new lintels have [[Buff (colour)|buff]]-coloured [[artificial stone]] facings.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}}

===1980s to 2000s: floodlighting and further repair===
[[File:Penshaw Monument (7022450619) (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|alt=Penshaw Monument lit in yellow at dusk|The monument illuminated in 2012]]

In 1982 a grant from the [[Countryside Commission]] allowed the National Trust to purchase {{convert|44|acres}} of land surrounding the monument,<ref name="henderson2005"/>{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=33}} including much of the south-facing side of Penshaw Hill and the woodland to the north-west.{{sfn|Newman|2019|p=2}} The monument has been illuminated at night by [[floodlight]]s since 1988, when Sunderland City Council paid £50,000 ({{Inflation|UK|50000|1988|r=-3|fmt=eq|cursign=£}}) for them.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} Between 1994 and 1996, several of these lights were stolen; [[Northumbria Police]] said they may have been used as [[grow light]]s for the [[Cannabis cultivation|cultivation of cannabis]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Millar |first1=Stuart |title=Church raid casts new light on skunk |work=The Guardian |date=9 November 1996 |page=6}}</ref> In 1994, ''[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]]'' reported that Penshaw Monument "might be sinking" after two surveys showed the levels of the bottoms of the columns were not even.<ref name="ffrench">{{cite news |last1={{Not a typo|Ffrench}} |first1=Andrew |title=Monument may have that sinking feeling |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0002240/19940819/020/0003 |work=Newcastle Journal |date=19 August 1994 |page=3| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> The National Trust hired the civil engineer Professor [[John Knapton (engineer)|John Knapton]] to carry out a third, more comprehensive survey of the monument to assess whether movement had occurred.<ref name="ffrench"/>

In 1996 the National Trust said it was spending over £100,000 ({{Inflation|UK|100000|1996|r=-3|fmt=eq|cursign=£}}) to restore the monument.<ref name="times1996">{{cite news |title=Restored folly will stay a mucky monument |work=[[The Times]] |date=8 August 1996 |page=18}}</ref> Its columns and lintels had deteriorated and were repaired.<ref name="times1996"/> Its [[cast iron]] [[wiktionary:cramp|cramps]] had [[rust]]ed, causing them to expand and stress the monument's stonework—these were replaced with [[stainless steel]] cramps bedded in [[lead]].<ref name="times1996"/> [[Repointing]] was done using [[lime mortar]] made from lime quarried at the National Trust's pits on the [[Wallington Hall|Wallington]] estate in [[Northumberland]].<ref name="times1996"/>{{efn|''The Times'' incorrectly writes that Wallington is in County Durham.}} However, the surface of the stone, which has been blackened by [[soot]], was not cleaned so that it would remain "a reminder of the area's tradition of heavy industry".<ref name="times1996"/> The Trust later said: "Now that mining has ceased, the blackness seems particularly evocative and proposals for cleaning have been resisted".<ref name="henderson2005"/> On 16 November 2005 a group of 60 volunteers, recruited by the National Trust from local businesses, universities and [[Boldon School]], [[South Tyneside]],<ref>{{cite news |title=Landmark folly gets a makeover |work=[[The Northern Echo]] |date=17 November 2005 |url=https://www.thenorthernecho.co.uk/news/7150259.landmark-folly-gets-makeover/ |access-date=10 August 2020}}</ref> assisted with the upkeep of the monument by replacing the [[kissing gate]] at its entrance, building a [[Pedestal|plinth]] and path for its [[Interpretation centre|interpretation area]], repairing and replacing the steps leading to it and planting {{convert|40|m}} of [[hedge]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Monumental effort, please |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=26 October 2005|page=52}}</ref>

===2010s and 2020s: reopening of the staircase and vandalism===
[[File:Penshaw Monument viewed from walkway at the top.JPG|thumb|upright=0.75|alt=View of the interior of Penshaw Monument from the walkway|The monument from the walkway]]
In August 2011 the National Trust opened the staircase to the public for the first time since 1926 and began to provide guided tours of the top of the structure.<ref name="bbc30aug2011">{{cite news |title=Penshaw Monument opening: National Trust turns hundreds away |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-tyne-14714058 |access-date=10 July 2020 |publisher=BBC News |date=30 August 2011}}</ref> More than 500 people attended the reopening, although only 75 were initially allowed to climb it; further tours took place in subsequent months.<ref name="bbc30aug2011"/> The trust now normally provides tours every weekend between [[Good Friday]] and the end of September.<ref>{{cite web |title=Take a stroll up Penshaw Hill |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/features/climb-penshaw-hill |publisher=[[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] |access-date=2 August 2020}}</ref> Tours last 15 minutes and only five people are allowed to go up at once; National Trust members can do so for free, but non-members must pay £5.<ref>{{cite web |title=Tours to the top |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/penshaw-monument/features/tours-to-the-top-of-penshaw-monument |publisher=[[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] |access-date=9 July 2020}}</ref> By March 2013, over 3600 people had taken the tours.<ref name="tallentire">{{cite news |last1=Tallentire |first1=Mark |title=Tours to top of Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.thenorthernecho.co.uk/news/10302455.tours-top-penshaw-monument/ |access-date=10 July 2020 |work=[[The Northern Echo]] |date=20 March 2013}}</ref>

In March 2014 the council announced that it would spend £43,000 to replace the floodlights<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-tyne-26503791|title=New lights for Penshaw Monument|date=9 March 2014|publisher=BBC News}}</ref> with 18 new [[Efficient energy use|energy-efficient]] [[LED lamp|LED lights]], which it expected would save £8000 per year in operating costs; they produce a softer, whiter light than their predecessors, and can be programmed to illuminate the monument in different colours.<ref name="diffley">{{cite news |last1=Diffley |first1=James |title=Penshaw Monument lit up by new £43,000 lighting scheme from Sunderland City Council |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/penshaw-monument-lit-up-new-9948862 |access-date=13 July 2020 |work=[[Evening Chronicle|Chronicle Live]] |date=28 August 2015}}</ref> They were paid for with a government loan and were expected to reduce annual energy consumption from 75,000 [[kilowatt-hour]]s to less than 10,000.<ref name="diffley"/> An archaeological [[watching brief]] was carried out during the lights' installation.{{sfn|Nicholson|2015|p=2}} In April 2015 nine of the new lights, worth £20,000, were stolen; thieves used [[bolt cutter]]s to breach the monument's security gates and open the lights' steel enclosures.<ref name="henderson">{{cite news |last1=Henderson |first1=Tony |title=Penshaw Monument floodlights stolen from iconic Wearside landmark |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/penshaw-monunent-floodlights-stolen-iconic-9104406 |access-date=13 July 2020 |work=[[Evening Chronicle|Chronicle Live]] |date=23 April 2015}}</ref> The installation was fully complete by August 2015.<ref name="diffley"/> The monument has since been illuminated in various colours to commemorate events, including the colours of the [[flag of France]] after the [[November 2015 Paris attacks]];<ref name="gillan"/> the colours of [[Hays Travel]] in November 2020 to commemorate [[John Hays (businessman)|John Hays]];<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-tyne-55061195 |title=Sunderland landmarks lit up in honour of travel firm boss |publisher=BBC News |date=24 November 2020 |access-date=24 February 2021}}</ref> the colours of the [[Union Jack]] on 31 January 2020 to mark [[Brexit]];<ref>{{cite news |title=The UK celebrates Brexit Day 2020, in pictures |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/politics/0/uk-celebrates-brexit-day-2020-pictures/sunderlands-famous-penshaw-monument-illuminated-red-white-blue0/ |access-date=31 July 2020 |work=The Daily Telegraph |date=31 January 2020}}</ref> and blue on 24 March 2020 as a tribute to [[National Health Service]] and [[Social work|social care]] workers during the [[COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Thompson |first1=Fiona |title=Sunderland's landmarks lit up in blue in support for NHS and social care workers leading coronavirus battle |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/health/coronavirus/sunderlands-landmarks-lit-blue-support-nhs-and-social-care-workers-leading-coronavirus-battle-2516592 |access-date=3 July 2020 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=26 April 2020}}</ref>

[[File:Steps to the Penshaw Monument (geograph 5719013).jpg|thumb|left|alt=Long wooden steps leading up a hill to Penshaw Monument|The old timber steps to the monument, pictured in 2018]]

On 26 July 2014, extensive areas of the monument were vandalised with red [[spray paint]].<ref name="bbc12aug">{{cite news |title=Graffiti removed after donations |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-tyne-28753444 |access-date=28 July 2019 |publisher=BBC News |date=12 August 2014}}</ref> The graffiti included text and symbols related to the 2005 film ''[[V for Vendetta (film)|V for Vendetta]]''.<ref name="bbc12aug"/> The National Trust said it had hired specialist contractors to remove the graffiti at a "considerable cost".<ref name="bbc30jul">{{cite web |title=Monument defaced with Vendetta signs |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-tyne-28570620 |publisher=BBC News |access-date=28 July 2019 |date=30 July 2014}}</ref> Although the Trust did not ask for donations, local residents and businesses gave £1,500 to pay for the removal.<ref name="bbc12aug"/> In 2019, as an [[April Fools' Day]] joke, it was announced that Penshaw Monument would be "moved stone by stone" to [[Beamish Museum]] in County Durham.<ref name="beamish">{{cite web |title=Penshaw Monument to be relocated to Beamish! Update: April Fool! |url=https://www.beamish.org.uk/news/penshaw-monument-to-beamish/ |publisher=[[Beamish Museum]] |access-date=10 July 2020}}</ref> The museum claimed the move was due to "the discovery of an infestation of a rare breed of worm in Penshaw Hill"—a reference to the [[Lambton Worm]] legend.<ref name="beamish"/>{{importance-inline}}

In August 2019 the National Trust received £200,000 from [[Highways England]]'s Environment Designated Fund.<ref name="henderson2019" /> It said it would use the money to provide better access to the monument by replacing old [[timber]] steps with new ones made of [[sandstone]] and limestone, and improve signage at the site using information from new [[Geological survey|ground surveys]].<ref name="nt2020">{{cite web |title=New project to improve access to Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/penshaw-monument/features/new-project-to-improve-access-to-penshaw-monument- |publisher=[[National Trust for Places of Historic Interest or Natural Beauty|National Trust]] |access-date=3 July 2020}}</ref> Repairs were also carried out on the monument itself, including stone repair and repointing on its stylobate.<ref name="nt2020"/> It was originally proposed to add a new spur path, and ditches at the side of the main path with [[Dry well|soakaway pits]] for [[drainage]]; however, these plans were abandoned, partly because of their archaeological impact.{{sfn|Newman|2019|p=7}} As part of the ''Lumiere in Durham'' light festival in November 2021, the monument is to be illuminated with 140,000 separate points of light to commemorate the UK dead in the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United Kingdom|COVID-19 pandemic]].<ref>{{cite web|url= https://www.theguardian.com/travel/2021/nov/18/lumiere-festival-of-light-durham-honours-covid-dead|title='A chariot in the sky':Lumiere festival in Durham honours Covid dead|first=Mark|last=Brown|work=The Guardian|date=18 November 2021}}</ref>

==Architecture==
[[File:Penshaw monument (6405902427).jpg|thumb|upright=1.2|alt=A monument in the style of a Greek temple, made of blackened stone|Penshaw Monument in 2011]]
Although it was intended as a memorial to the Earl of Durham, many sources describe Penshaw Monument as a [[folly]];{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}}<ref>{{cite news |last1=Taylor |first1=Louise |title=Folly of Sunderland contrasts starkly with efficiency of Southampton |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2015/may/01/sunderland-folly-southampton-revenge-danger-drop |access-date=19 June 2020 |work=The Guardian |date=1 May 2015}}</ref>{{sfn|Jackson|2019|p=101}} it features no statue or sculpture of the man it was intended to commemorate.{{sfn|''Companion to the Almanac''|1845|p=225}}{{efn|There is a popular story that the statue of the Marquess of Londonderry in the market square in Durham was intended to be placed on top of Penshaw Monument; this is not true.<ref name="seddon">{{cite news |last1=Seddon |first1=Sean |title=Penshaw Monument: Was it supposed to have a roof? We look at the myths and legends |url=https://www.chroniclelive.co.uk/news/north-east-news/penshaw-monument-supposed-roof-look-12223280 |access-date=18 June 2020 |work=[[Evening Chronicle|Chronicle Live]] |date=26 November 2016}}</ref>}} The monument was built in the style of a [[Ancient Greek temple|temple]] of the [[Doric order]].<ref name="he"/> It is based on the [[Temple of Hephaestus]], which is on the [[Agoraios Kolonos]] hill on the north-west side of the [[Ancient Agora of Athens]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=46}} The National Trust describes it as a [[replica]] of the temple;<ref name="nt">{{cite web |title=Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.nationaltrust.org.uk/penshaw-monument#Overview |publisher=[[National Trust]] |access-date=19 June 2020}}</ref> however, according to the ''Sunderland Echo'', "at best it could be said it is 'slightly similar to' the Temple of Hephaestus".<ref name="gillan"/>{{efn|The monument is a similar size to the Temple of Hephaestus, but has a simpler design with fewer columns;{{sfn|Middleton|2010|pp=46–47}} the temple has thirteen by six columns.<ref name="he"/> The monument's height is roughly double that of the temple, as is the diameter of its columns.<ref>{{cite news |title=Penshaw Monument |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000640/19480605/026/0002 |work=[[Sunderland Echo|Sunderland Echo and Shipping Gazette]] |date=5 June 1948 |page=2| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref>}}

It is an example of [[Greek Revival architecture]], which is rare in the historic County Durham.{{sfn|Pevsner|Williamson|1983|p=44}} The style first appeared there {{circa|1820}} at [[English country house|country houses]] like [[Eggleston Hall]]; Penshaw Monument is a late example, as is [[Monkwearmouth Station Museum|Monkwearmouth Railway Station]].{{sfn|Pevsner|Williamson|1983|p=44}} [[John Martin Robinson]] cites the monument alongside [[Bowes Museum]] as an example of the "eccentric buildings" found in the county.{{sfn|Robinson|1986|p=93}} [[Nikolaus Pevsner]] noted that the structure's proximity to the [[Victoria Viaduct]] produces a rare juxtaposition of Greek and Roman architecture.{{sfn|Pevsner|Williamson|1983|p=44}}{{efn|A third edition of the [[Pevsner Architectural Guides]], ''County Durham: Buildings of England'', coauthored by Martin Roberts with Nikolaus Pevsner and Elizabeth Williamson, was published in 2021. It has an illustration of Penshaw Monument on its cover and an entry for the monument on page 565.{{sfn|Roberts|Pevsner|Williamson|2021|p=565}}}} A booklet produced by [[Tyne & Wear Archives & Museums|Tyne and Wear County Council Museums]] compares Penshaw Monument to [[Jesmond Old Cemetery]], whose gates were designed by [[John Dobson (architect)|John Dobson]]; it says that the monument "shares the [[Arcadia (utopia)|Arcadian]] intentions of Dobson's Cemetery, but is very much more successful".{{sfn|Tyne and Wear County Council Museums|1980|p=24}} In her survey of the monument commissioned by the National Trust, Penny Middleton states that its "closest architectural and cultural relation" may be the [[National Monument of Scotland]], an unfinished Grecian temple on [[Calton Hill]] in [[Edinburgh]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=52}}

===Description===
[[File:Detail (6405902063).jpg|thumb|left|upright=0.75|alt=Close-up of blackened columns supporting the entablature|Detail of the columns]]

Penshaw Monument is {{convert|100|ft|order=flip}} long, {{convert|53|ft|order=flip|abbr=on}} wide and {{convert|70|ft|order=flip|abbr=on}} high,{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} making it the biggest structure serving only as a memorial in North East England.{{sfn|Usherwood|Beach|Morris|2000|p=166}} It is made of [[gritstone]] [[ashlar]],<ref name="he"/>{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}}{{efn|Gritstone was chosen because local [[sandstone]] would not be durable enough to survive weather conditions on the hill.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}}}} which was yellow at first, but has darkened.{{sfn|Usherwood|Beach|Morris|2000|p=166}} The stone was originally held together by steel pins and brackets.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} Graffiti is present on many areas of the monument, in the form of both carvings and ink.{{sfn|Usherwood|Beach|Morris|2000|p=166}} Its foundations originally sat on [[limestone]] {{convert|20|ft|order=flip|abbr=on}} below the ground.<ref name="times1844">{{cite news |title=Monument to the Late Earl of Durham |work=[[The Times]] |date=30 August 1844 |page=5 |url=https://www.thetimes.com/archive/article/1844-08-30/5/8.html}}</ref> The base consists of the upper [[stylobate]] and the lower stereobate<ref name="elevation">{{cite web |title=Elevation survey examples: Penshaw Monument |url=https://amrgeomatics.co.uk/penshaw-elevation.html |publisher=AMR Geomatics |access-date=12 August 2020}}</ref>—the columns sit on the stylobate, which is made of large gritstone blocks.<ref name="he"/>{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}} The height of the base varies from {{convert|1.23|m|abbr=on}} at the south-west corner to {{convert|2.35|m|abbr=on}} at the south-east.<ref name="elevation"/> When the monument was built there were no steps leading up to the stylobate.{{sfn|Newman|2019|p=4}} The floor consists of [[Sett (paving)|setts]],{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}} which are pointed in [[Mortar (masonry)|mortar]] and laid to falls{{efn|"Laid to falls" means that the stones are on a slight slope to allow rainwater to drain along them.}} to diagonal [[flagstones]].{{sfn|RNJ Partnership|2019|p=1}} These flagstones direct rainwater to a central gulley.{{sfn|RNJ Partnership|2019|p=1}}

The monument is a [[Portico#Tetrastyle|tetrastyle]] structure.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}} It has 18 tapered, [[Fluting (architecture)|unfluted]] columns:{{sfn|Usherwood|Beach|Morris|2000|p=166}}{{sfn|Pevsner|Williamson|1983|p=371}} seven along the north- and south-facing sides and four facing the east and west.<ref name="elevation"/> The columns are {{convert|6|ft|6|in|order=flip|abbr=on}} in diameter,{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} and {{convert|10|m|abbr=on}} high.<ref name="elevation"/> There are two [[parapet]]ed walkways{{efn|The walkways are called "promenades" in nineteenth-century sources.{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}}{{sfn|''Art Union''|1844}}{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}}}} running from east to west at the top of the monument;<ref name="roof">{{cite web |title=Measured building survey examples: Penshaw Monument |url=https://amrgeomatics.co.uk/penshaw-floorplan.html |publisher=AMR Geomatics |access-date=12 August 2020}}</ref> the parapets are {{convert|3|ft|order=flip|abbr=on}} tall.<ref name="henderson2011">{{cite news |last1=Henderson |first1=Tony |title=Monumental views |work=[[Evening Chronicle]] |date=26 August 2011 |page=23}}</ref> One column—the second from the east on the south-facing side<ref name="topographical">{{cite web |title=Topographical survey examples: Penshaw Monument |url=https://amrgeomatics.co.uk/penshaw-monument.html |publisher=AMR Geomatics |access-date=12 August 2020}}</ref>—contains a 74-step<ref name="tallentire"/> [[Stairs#Spiral and helical stairs|spiral staircase]] leading to the southern walkway.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=2}}<ref name="roof"/> The columns support a deep [[entablature]], whose [[wiktionary:blocking course|blocking course]] serves as the walkways;<ref name="times1844"/>{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}} the columns, and the walls of the [[Foundation (engineering)|foundation]] and entablature, are hollow.{{efn|According to Sunderland City Council, the 17 columns that do not contain the staircase are solid;{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=2}} however, other sources agree that they are hollow.{{sfn|Fordyce|1857|p=567}}{{sfn|Usherwood|Beach|Morris|2000|p=166}}{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}}}} The entablature is made up of the [[architrave]], [[frieze]] and [[cornice]]; the architrave and cornice are simple in design, and the frieze is adorned with [[triglyph]]s, although these are stylised and lack grooves.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}} There is a triangular [[pediment]] at each end of the entablature;{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}} the total height of the entablature and pediments is {{convert|6.62|m|abbr=on}}.<ref name="elevation"/>

[[File:Monument detail (6405902225).jpg|thumb|alt=A view from inside the monument, showing it has no roof|Detail of the entablature]]

The structure has no roof,<ref name="he"/> leading ''[[The Illustrated London News]]'' to call it [[hypaethral]];{{sfn|''Illustrated London News''|1844|p=149}} however, ''The Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal'' considered this adjective inappropriate.{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}} It asserted that "[a]n 'hypoethral' temple does not mean one without any roof at all{{nbsp}}... but only that peculiar kind of temple in which the ''cella'' was left partly uncovered"—the monument has no [[cella]].{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}} ''The Athenaeum'' called it "not only hypaethral, but hypaethral ''in issimo''{{efn|''In [[wiktionary:-issimo#Latin|issimo]]'' means "to the greatest possible degree".}}—and after the most extraordinary fashion", and remarked, "Possibly it was at first intended that there should be a roof, but in order to save expense, it was afterwards thought that such covering might be dispensed with".{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}} According to the council, the monument was indeed originally intended to have a roof and interior walls but these were never built due to a lack of funding.{{sfn|Sunderland City Council|2015|p=1}} However, ''[[Evening Chronicle|The Chronicle]]'' has reported that this is a myth and a roof was never planned.<ref name="seddon"/>{{efn|There is a myth that the monument lacks a roof because the Earl of Durham increased his tenants' rent during its construction, causing them to refuse to build it.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=47}} In reality, construction began four years after his death.}}

==Reception and impact==
===19th century===
[[File:Durham Monument Nicholson.png|thumb|upright=1.3|left|alt=An engraving of an oversized Greek temple on a hill|An engraving of the monument made shortly after its erection, which shows it out of proportion with the hill{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=39}}]]
Before its completion, the ''Carlisle Journal'' said Penshaw Monument would be "one of England's proudest architectural wonders, and a fitting memorial of one of its wisest statesmen".<ref name="carlisle"/> However, its design initially met with a hostile response in architectural circles: in 1844 ''The Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal'' called it "as arrant a piece of 'nonsense architecture' as can well be imagined" and derisively compared it to a [[Animal pound|cattle pound]].{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}} ''[[The Art Union]]'' was similarly scathing: it was critical of the idea of a Greek temple, which it said "does not bespeak much of either invention or judgement". It concluded: "To us it appears to be one of the most absurd, ill-imagined, and ill-contrived things ever devised, utterly devoid of significancy, purpose, or meaning."{{sfn|''Art Union''|1844}}

''The Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal'' termed the presence of pediments despite the absence of a roof a "gross and palpable violation of meaning and common sense".{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}} ''[[The Athenaeum (British magazine)|The Athenaeum]]'' was similarly critical of the roofless pediments—"the building will look as if there had originally been a roof to it, which had fallen in!"—and expressed disapproval of the lack of cella and inner walls, writing that the architects had "cut the [[Gordian knot]]" by omitting them.{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}} The decision to make the walls and columns hollow was condemned by ''The Athenaeum'', which called the structure "nearly as much a sham, as if it were composed of cast iron coloured in imitation of stone" and said it "may be intended as characteristic{{nbsp}}... of an age which estimates plausible appearances above solid worth".{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}} ''The Art Union'' agreed: the hollowness of the columns, it said, "partakes too much of sham construction, with little if any thing to recommend it on the score of economy".{{sfn|''Art Union''|1844}}

''The Athenaeum'' wrote that climbing the monument's stairs could be dangerous,{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}} and ''The Art Union'' condemned the staircase as "dreadfully narrow and inconvenient".{{sfn|''Art Union''|1844}} ''The Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal'' believed that those who ascended the monument would "have to promenade somewhat after the fashion of crows in a gutter", and suggested that the architects ought to have provided a terrace on the roof of the monument, accessible by a wider staircase.{{sfn|''Civil Engineer and Architect's Journal''|1844|p=373}} ''The Athenaeum'' expressed confusion at the absence of a statue or commemoration of the Earl of Durham, believing that "although it may not yet be definitely settled what it is to be, something must surely be intended".{{sfn|''Athenaeum''|1844|p=836}}

In 1850 ''The Times'' was more complimentary: "The position and architecture of this structure are both extremely fine, and viewed from the railway it produces the best effect."<ref>{{cite news |title=The royal progress to Scotland |work=[[The Times]] |date=30 August 1850 |page=4 |url=https://www.thetimes.com/archive/article/1850-08-30/4/6.html}}</ref> At the [[Great Exhibition]], which took place in [[the Crystal Palace]] in [[Hyde Park, London]] in 1851, a model of the monument made of [[cannel coal]] was exhibited as part of the event's Mining and Metallurgy section.{{sfn|Hunt|1851|p=18}} In 1857 William Fordyce wrote: "The temple is remarkable for its grandeur, simplicity and imposing effect, nothing in the shape of ornament or meretricious decoration being introduced".{{sfn|Fordyce|1857|p=567}} In his [[graphic novel]] ''[[Alice in Sunderland]]'', which explores [[Lewis Carroll]]'s connections to Sunderland, [[Bryan Talbot]] suggests that Carroll may have been inspired by the monument, comparing the door leading to the monument's stairs to a scene omitted from ''[[Alice's Adventures in Wonderland]]'' (1865), in which [[Alice (Alice's Adventures in Wonderland)|Alice]] knocks on a door in a tree.{{sfn|Talbot|2014|p=226}} In his 1887 story "The Flower of Weardale", author [[William Delisle Hay]] described the monument:

{{blockquote|Upon the crest of a bare and ugly eminence that towers above the [[River Wear|Wear]], there stands a mighty experiment in stone: a Grecian temple, splendid and solemn, with its columns and entablatures, yet blackened and stained by the sooty atmosphere, and looming grandly through the rolling smoke-clouds rising from the collieries down below, over which it lords, and with which it has no fellowship.

Queer art, curious taste, surely, which has raised this majestic memorial of an aesthetic age and clime, and planted it, severe and solid, here in the centre of the now most prosaic county of practical, toiling, prosaic England! A monstrous monument, set up a few decades ago in honour of some notability, dead and in spite of it forgotten! Yet may this modern Durham folly on Penshaw Hill emblematize the passage of the centuries, and serve, at any rate, to indicate to us a spot whereby there hangs a legend of elder England.{{sfn|Hay|1887|p=673}}}}

===20th and 21st centuries===
[[File:Penshaw Monument White Band Day 1st July 2005.jpg|thumb|upright=1.3|alt=An aerial view of Penshaw Monument, surrounded by hundreds of people in white clothing|People forming a "living white band" around the monument in July 2005 to promote the [[Make Poverty History]] campaign]]
The monument is often described by recent sources as a [[landmark]] which indicates to locals that they have returned home after a long journey;<ref name="hansard"/><ref name="henderson2005"/><ref>{{cite news |title=More landmarks to show you're nearly home |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-41124879 |access-date=10 July 2020 |publisher=BBC News |date=10 September 2017}}</ref> it is no longer widely associated with John Lambton.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=52}} It is a key part of Sunderland's [[cultural identity]],{{sfn|Newman|2019|p=6}} frequently depicted by local photographers and artists.{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=39}} Nationally, it is viewed as a symbol of the North East alongside the bridges of the [[River Tyne]] and the [[Angel of the North]].{{sfn|Middleton|2010|p=51}} [[Sunderland A.F.C.]]'s current [[Crest (sports)|crest]], adopted in 1997, features a depiction of Penshaw Monument; according to [[Bob Murray (businessman)|Bob Murray]], the club's chairman at the time, it was included "to acknowledge the depth of support for the team outside the City boundaries".<ref>{{cite news |title=Sir Bob Murray recalls why he changed Sunderland AFC badge after 'sinking ship' ridicule |url=https://www.sunderlandecho.com/news/sir-bob-murray-recalls-why-he-changed-sunderland-afc-badge-after-sinking-ship-ridicule-232816 |access-date=3 July 2020 |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=31 October 2018}}</ref>{{efn|The monument is in the modern City of Sunderland district; the ''Sunderland Echo'' interprets this remark as a reference to its visibility from afar.<ref name="gillan"/>}}

In 2006 the monument featured alongside [[Hadrian's Wall]] and the [[Sage Gateshead]] in a [[television advertisement]] for the "Passionate People, Passionate Places" campaign, which was intended to promote North East England.<ref name="rouse">{{cite news |last1=Rouse |first1=Stephen |title=Film's top guns to put North name in lights |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=17 January 2006 |page=7}}</ref> It was produced by RSA Films, a company founded by the directors [[Tony Scott|Tony]] and [[Ridley Scott]], both natives of the North East.<ref name="rouse"/> In 2007 the structure came second in a poll of places which inspired the most pride in residents of the region, behind [[Durham Cathedral]] and [[Durham Castle|Castle]];<ref>{{cite news |title=Landmark tops region 'proud' poll |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/england/wear/7127118.stm |access-date=9 July 2020 |publisher=BBC News |date=5 December 2007}}</ref> in a survey conducted by the advertising company CBS Outdoor, 59% of people from Sunderland said Penshaw Monument was an important landmark.<ref>{{cite news |title=Mackem pride tops British |work=[[Sunderland Echo]] |date=11 July 2007}}</ref> It appears in [[Richard T. Kelly]]'s 2008 novel ''[[Crusaders (novel)|Crusaders]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Whetstone |first1=David |title=North-East thrust into limelight |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=27 December 2007 |url=http://www.thejournal.co.uk/culture/arts/north-east-thrust-into-limelight-4707143 |access-date=10 August 2020 |archive-date=22 September 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150922150240/http://www.thejournal.co.uk/culture/arts/north-east-thrust-into-limelight-4707143 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The National Trust has said that since it reopened the structure's staircase in 2011, "for some making it to the top has become a sort of personal pilgrimage, with many visitors finding it an inspiring and often quite emotional experience".<ref>{{cite news |title=High times expected as monument opens again |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=27 March 2012 |page=3}}</ref>

[[File:View from Penshaw Monument over surrounding countryside - geograph.org.uk - 101872.jpg|thumb|left|alt=View of the sky and surrounding countryside from inside Penshaw Monument|View from inside the monument]]

[[Bryan Ferry]], lead singer of the band [[Roxy Music]], grew up in Washington, and often visited Penshaw Hill with his father.{{sfn|Ferry|2008}} He told ''The Times'' that the monument made a significant impression on him as a child, and seemed "like a symbol{{nbsp}}... representing art, and another life, away from the coal fields and the hard Northeastern environment; it seemed to represent something from another civilization, that was much finer".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Bracewell |first1=Michael |author-link1=Michael Bracewell (writer) |title=The birth of Roxy Music |url=https://www.thetimes.com/sunday-times-rich-list/profile/article/the-birth-of-roxy-music-k08bv98ffbm |access-date=4 July 2020 |work=[[The Times]] |date=13 October 2007 |url-access=subscription}}</ref> He has conceded, however, that the monument is "essentially a folly, a building without purpose".{{sfn|Ferry|2008}} In his book on Roxy Music, [[Michael Bracewell (writer)|Michael Bracewell]] describes the experience of approaching the monument: "its immensity drawing nearer, the heroic ideal of the place falls away. Grandeur gives way to mere enormity, statement to silence, substance to emptiness.{{nbsp}}... [T]his solid memorial{{nbsp}}... was designed, like stage scenery, to be appreciated from a distance."{{sfn|Bracewell|2008|p=11}} 
[[File:Penshaw Monument Autumn Sunset.webm|thumb|alt=Panoramic video of Penshaw Monument and the surrounding hill and fields|[[Aerial photography|Drone footage]] of Penshaw Monument at dusk]]

[[Alan Robson]] called the monument "a striking example of Doric architecture" in an article for the ''[[Evening Chronicle]]''.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Robson |first1=Alan |author-link1=Alan Robson |title=You'll never feel lonely in this town |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0000726/19650222/136/0007 |work=[[Evening Chronicle]] |date=22 February 1965 |page=III| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> ''[[The Independent]]''{{'s}} James Wilson thought it "perplexing" and a "seriously silly folly".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Wilson |first1=James |title=A city red and white and proud all over – Britain's newest city, Sunderland |work=The Independent |date=13 June 1992 |page=48}}</ref> ''The Journal''{{'s}} Tony Jones called it "a visible but under-rated symbol of regional identity".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Jones |first1=Tony |title=Folly's anniversary set to pass quietly |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/BL/0002240/19940124/195/0019 |work=[[The Journal (Newcastle upon Tyne newspaper)|The Journal]] |date=24 January 1994 |page=41| via = British Newspaper Archive|url-access=subscription}}</ref> David Brandon described it in ''[[The Guardian]]'' as an "extraordinary{{nbsp}}... symbol of the Earl of Durham's insanity and county pride".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Brandon |first1=David |title=A cry for a lost childhood |work=The Guardian |date=11 August 1997 |page=8}}</ref> ''[[The Northern Echo]]''{{'s}} Chris Lloyd compared Penshaw Monument to the Angel of the North, calling both "beautifully pointless".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lloyd |first1=Chris |title=A heavenly host greets the Angel |work=[[The Northern Echo]] |date=17 February 1998 |page=8}}</ref> Tony Henderson, also of ''The Journal'', wrote that the lack of roof and interior walls "has been to the advantage of the monument as it allows the dramatic play of light among the columns".<ref name="henderson2005"/> In her ''Monument Guide to England and Wales'', Jo Darke called the monument "a great northern landmark";{{sfn|Darke|1991|pp=231–232}} [[architectural historian]]s [[Gwyn Headley]] and Wim Meulenkamp have described its blackened surface as "a satanic response to the pure white Hellenic ideal".{{sfn|Headley|Meulenkamp|1999|pp=221–222}} Nikolaus Pevsner wrote that "the monument looms as an apparition of the [[Acropolis of Athens|Acropolis]] under [[Hyperborea|hyperborian]] skies".{{sfn|Pevsner|Williamson|1983|p=44}} In his book ''The Northumbrians'', historian Dan Jackson praised it: "It is a building of great gravitas, and its austere Doric silhouette dominates the landscape for miles around."{{sfn|Jackson|2019|p=101}}

